	'ms_lede' => 'Our in-house model shop in Haverholt crafts hand-shaped foam iterations, precise resin prints, and painted appearance models. Working directly with physical matter allows us to test weight, grip, and proportion long before digital data is locked.',
	'ms_m1' => 'Foam · sanded by hand',
	'ms_m2' => 'Resin · printed and finished',
	'ms_n0' => 'Three kinds of model',
	'ms_p0' => 'The model shop is the physical heart of Tsukuri Design Office. While digital CAD tools provide geometric precision, human hands remain the ultimate judges of ergonomic comfort, warmth, and balance. We work across three primary physical media: fast hand-sanded polyurethane foam blocks for volumetric exploration, high-resolution resin prints for fine mechanical snaps and internal chassis verification, and weighted, fully painted appearance models finished with production-grade textures. Shaping prototypes by hand lets our designers evaluate thumb clearance, parting line comfort, and visual heft in minutes rather than waiting weeks for third-party rapid prototyping bureaus.',
	'ms_p1' => 'Kofi Asante leads our model shop, bringing deep craftsmanship in foam shaping, precision sanding, and multi-part resin casting. Having guided more than one hundred physical prototypes across our studio\'s history, Kofi ensures that every model leaving the bench communicates clear tactile information to clients and end users. Working alongside materials specialist Sofia Lindqvist, he formulates custom surface sheens and balanced internal ballast so physical prototypes feel like genuine factory-finished goods. This immediate workshop dialogue between industrial designers and modelmakers prevents miscommunication and accelerates design iterations across all project stages.',
	'ms_p2' => 'We welcome client engineering teams, product managers, and design partners to our Haverholt model shop on Tuesdays and Thursdays between 10:00 and 16:00, strictly by appointment. Because our workshop involves active foam carving, sanding dust extraction, and resin post-curing operations, we schedule dedicated visiting slots to ensure visitor safety and focused discussion. During a shop visit, guests can handle historical case study models, review ongoing ergonomic iterations at the workbench, and examine physical CMF material palettes alongside our makers. Appointments can be scheduled by contacting studio manager Paul Moreau.',
