	'sp_desc' => 'One discovery day, €2,400, and a reply within 3 working days.',
	'sp_h0' => 'The discovery workshop structure and outcomes',
	'sp_h1' => 'Booking terms and project scheduling with Paul Moreau',
	'sp_h2' => 'What should teams prepare before the discovery workshop day?',
	'sp_l0' => 'The discovery day',
	'sp_l1' => 'Write to us',
	'sp_lede' => 'Every project at Tsukuri Design Office begins with a one-day discovery workshop at our Haverholt studio or your premises. This focused session aligns technical feasibility, user needs, and commercial objectives before design begins.',
	'sp_n0' => 'The day',
	'sp_n1' => 'Booking',
	'sp_n2' => 'Bring with you',
	'sp_p0' => 'The single way to start a project with our team is through our discovery workshop, priced at a fixed fee of €2,400. Conducted over one intensive day at our Haverholt studio or your company offices, this session brings your core stakeholders together with design director Ines Varga, mechanical engineer Mateo Ruiz, and studio manager Paul Moreau. We dismantle existing prototypes, review competitive benchmarks, examine regulatory constraints, and define the critical ergonomic boundaries of your product. Within 5 working days following the workshop, we deliver a written problem statement, technical risk assessment, and precise project roadmap.',
	'sp_p1' => 'Because we restrict our studio capacity to at most nine client engagements each year, we schedule discovery workshops deliberately to preserve quality. To initiate a project, email your overview to studio@tsukuri.example or call our studio directly at +00 31 555 0172. Studio manager Paul Moreau personally reviews every enquiry and replies within 3 working days offering two proposed workshop dates. Should you proceed into subsequent design and prototyping stages following the session, the full €2,400 discovery fee is credited against your next project invoice, providing immediate value toward your ongoing development program.',
	'sp_p2' => 'To make the most of our one-day session, participating teams should gather any existing early CAD files, competitive market samples, bill-of-materials targets, and relevant user feedback. If you have already built rough proof-of-concept models or encountered manufacturing failures with previous vendors, bring those physical parts along. We examine physical failures closely to identify root causes in wall geometry, material selection, or assembly ergonomics. Having key commercial and technical decision-makers in the room ensures that our resulting problem statement provides an accurate, actionable foundation for subsequent concept development and physical prototyping.',
