	'wk_l_d' => 'Case D',
	'wk_lede' => 'We take consumer products and medical devices from initial foam models to verified production tooling. Below are four recent projects documenting how physical prototyping resolves critical functional problems before manufacturing begins.',
	'wk_p0' => 'Developed for a hospital equipment maker, the Cradle handset is a regulated medical device designed to solve two persistent bedside failures. In active hospital wards, night nurses could not find the call button by touch in low light, while resting patients repeatedly dropped the handset off bedside rails. Over fourteen months from project brief to first tool sample, our team shaped forty-one successive hand-sanded foam prototypes to define an unmistakable thumb depression and an integrated rail-hook balance. Finished in March 2025, the production device combines a soft navy primary call button with a matte white enclosure made for daily clinical cleaning.',
	'wk_p1' => 'The Mora kettle is a 1.2 litre electric kettle developed for an established kitchen appliance maker. The original concept suffered from two severe usability flaws: steam venting toward the grip burned knuckles during pouring, and users could not unlatch the top lid with one wet hand while holding the vessel under a kitchen tap. Across eleven months of development, our workshop tested twenty-three foam and cardboard handle iterations to reposition the steam path and refine the mechanical latch geometry. Completed in October 2024, the resulting production kettle delivers balanced one-handed operation and cool-touch handling without adding unnecessary mechanical complexity.',
	'wk_p2' => 'Commissioned by a pharmaceutical device supplier, the Tally dose pen is a reusable medical injection pen engineered for patients managing stiff fingers and reduced hand dexterity. The initial mechanism proved unworkable for arthritic hands because patients could neither read the narrow dose window nor grip the slender dial to set their prescribed medication units. Over eighteen months leading to the first tool sample in June 2025, we evaluated fifty-six hand-sanded models to validate an enlarged tactile dial, high-contrast numerical spacing, and positive internal ratchets. The final assembly ensures clear audible feedback and effortless dosing without requiring excessive finger pinch strength.',
	'wk_p3' => 'Created for an audio start-up, the Pell portable speaker required a complete mechanical re-engineering after early prototype quotations arrived at twice the client\'s production budget. In addition to high unit costs, the initial concept relied on messy adhesive bonds that slowed factory assembly lines and precluded easy servicing or material recycling. Working through eighteen physical prototypes over nine months, our engineering team eliminated all internal glue joints in favour of interlocking molded snaps and four accessible mechanical fasteners. Finished in January 2025, the compact speaker met strict unit targets while integrating a durable mauve-grey carry strap and clean exterior surfaces.',
	'work_all' => 'See all work →',
);
